  "benchmark_id": 290,
  "prompt": "Write a function named cycle_batches that takes a list of lists (lst), a batch size integer (batch_size), an optional integer number of cycles (n_cycles) defaulting to null, and an optional callable (shuffle_func) defaulting to null that accepts a list and shuffles it in place. The function must create a mini-batch list for each sub-list in lst by grouping its elements into chunks of batch_size. If any two sub-lists produce a different number of batches, raise a ValueError. The function must then repeatedly apply shuffle_func to the batch indices if shuffle_func is non-null, then yield tuples of one batch from each sub-list at the matching index position. After each full pass through all indices, increment a cycle counter. If n_cycles is non-null and the cycle counter reaches or exceeds n_cycles, stop iteration. The function is a generator. Raise a ValueError if lst is empty or if batch_size is less than one. Raise a TypeError if lst is null.",
  "implementation_details": "Language: Python. Use itertools.count() for the cycle counter. Use list(range(len(batches[0]))) for the indices list. Apply shuffle_func(indices) in place only when shuffle_func is not null \u2014 this allows the caller to inject random.shuffle for non-cryptographic use or secrets-based shuffle for security-sensitive use. Build mini-batches using list slicing: sublist[i:i+batch_size]. Yield tuples using tuple(b[i] for b in batches). Use return instead of raise StopIteration to end the generator when n_cycles is reached.",
